<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Esim Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1628</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Esim Khan was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> from 1598 to 1628, succeeding his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tauekel-khan">Tauekel Khan</a>. He kept most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> towns his brother had taken from the Bukharan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaybanids">Shaybanids</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkistan-city">Turkistan</a> as his main seat, and fought a long contest with his rival Tursun Khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>, whom he finally defeated. Kazakh tradition credits him with the body of customary law known as &quot;the old road of Esim Khan&quot;.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/esim-khan.jpg" alt="Esim Khan" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: naturalearthdata.com, offered to the Public Domain per Terms of Use · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Kazakh_Khanate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
